|~ What is declared under `::set`: the refusal of its constructor, and
   the verbs of sets, each under the contract of its name on `::any`,
   whose page and laws it answers. A set is the vector in the one order
   without duplicates: a verb that keeps its subject's kind answers a
   set through the constructor of `::set`, and one that imposes an order
   answers the vector. ~|

:set/PayloadNotVecError
  |~~ `::set` builds a set from a vector, `::set[3 1 3]`; a payload of
      another kind is refused.

      ~(
        ::set{:not :a-vec}
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::set/PayloadNotVecError ::map]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:set/count
  |~~ The number of elements. ::holds~(is ::number)

      ~(#[:a :b] | count | eq 2)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/empty
  |~~ Whether the set holds no element. ::holds~(is ::boolean)

      ~(#[] | empty | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/first
  |~~ The least element, or null for the empty set.

      ~(#[:c :a :b] | first | eq :a)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/last
  |~~ The greatest element, or null for the empty set.

      ~(#[:c :a :b] | last | eq :c)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/sum
  |~~ The sum of the elements, each a number. ::holds~(is ::number)

      ~(#[1 2 3] | sum | eq 6)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/avg
  |~~ The mean of the elements, each a number, or null when there is none.

      ~(#[1 2 6] | avg | eq 3)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/min
  |~~ The least element, or the one whose key answers the least value.

      ~(#[3 1 4] | min | eq 1)
      ~(#[3 1 4] | min ~(desc) | eq 4)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/max
  |~~ The greatest element, or the one whose key answers the greatest
      value.

      ~(#[3 1 4] | max | eq 4)
      ~(#[3 1 4] | max ~(desc) | eq 1)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/filter
  |~~ The set of the elements whose predicate answers true. ::keeps~()

      ~(#[1 2 3 4 5] | filter ~(gt 2) | eq #[3 4 5])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/every
  |~~ Whether the predicate answers true for every element. ::holds~(is ::boolean)

      ~(#[2 4] | every ~(gt 0) | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/any
  |~~ Whether the predicate answers true for some element. ::holds~(is ::boolean)

      ~(#[1 2 3] | any ~(gt 2) | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/groupBy
  |~~ The map of sets the key sorts the elements into. ::holds~(is ::map)

      ~(#[1 2 3] | groupBy ~(if (gt 1) ~(:big) ~(:small)) | /big | eq #[2 3])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/indexBy
  |~~ The map of the elements under the key each answers, or under
      itself without a key. ::holds~(is ::map)

      ~(#[{:id :a}] | indexBy ~(/id) | /a | eq {:id :a})
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/sort
  |~~ The vector of the elements in the one order, or by the value the
      key answers for each; the order is imposed, so the answer is the
      vector. ::holds~(is ::vec)

      ~(#[:y :x :z] | sort | eq [:x :y :z])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/take
  |~~ The set of the least count elements. ::keeps~()

      ~(#[:d :c :b :a] | take 2 | eq #[:a :b])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/drop
  |~~ The set of the elements after the least count. ::keeps~()

      ~(#[:d :c :b :a] | drop 2 | eq #[:c :d])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/at
  |~~ The element at the index in the one order, a negative one
      counting from the end, or null past either end.

      ~(#[:a :b :c] | at -1 | eq :c)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/distinct
  |~~ The set itself. ::keeps~()

      ~(#[1 2 3] | distinct | eq #[1 2 3])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/reverse
  |~~ The vector of the elements in the reverse of the one order. ::holds~(is ::vec)

      ~(#[:a :b :c] | reverse | eq [:c :b :a])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/flat
  |~~ The set of the elements with one level of nesting spliced in, so
      a set of sets flattens into their union. ::keeps~()

      ~(#[#[1 2] #[2 3]] | flat | eq #[1 2 3])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/reduce
  |~~ The seed folded over the elements in the one order by the reducer.

      ~(#[1 2 3] | reduce 0 ~(add) | eq 6)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/union
  |~~ The set of the elements of either set, merged in the one order;
      left without the other set, the union of the sets the set holds. ::keeps~()

      ~(#[:a :b] | union #[:b :c] | eq #[:a :b :c])
      ~(#[#[1] #[2]] | union | eq #[1 2])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/union/with
  |~~ The value the subject is joined with, null when it gives none. ::holds~(is ::set ::null) ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:set/minus
  |~~ The set of the elements the other set lacks. ::keeps~()

      ~(#[:a :b :c] | minus #[:b] | eq #[:a :c])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/minus/with
  |~~ The value the subject is joined with, null when it gives none. ::holds~(is ::set ::null) ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:set/inter
  |~~ The set of the elements both sets hold. ::keeps~()

      ~(#[:a :b :c] | inter #[:b :d] | eq #[:b])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/inter/with
  |~~ The value the subject is joined with, null when it gives none. ::holds~(is ::set ::null) ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:set/has
  |~~ Whether the set holds the element, found by a binary search in
      the one order. ::holds~(is ::boolean)

      ~(#[:a :b :c] | has :b | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:set/contains
  |~~ Whether the set holds the element, the membership `has` asks,
      under the word a string answers for its substrings. ::holds~(is ::boolean)

      ~(#[:a :b :c] | contains :b | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}
